Some background on the test setup: I'm using a Broadcom reference=20
design AP platform with an BRCM 4708 host SOC. For the AP router=20
platform the opensource packet OpenWRT was used. Some small
modifications were made to get it to work on our HW. Only one core
is enabled for the moment (no time to figure out how to enable the
other one). Openwrt was configured to use kernel 3.18-rc2 and=20
the brcmfmac of the compat-wireless code was updated with our=20
latest code (minor patches, which have been submitted already).=20
The device used is 43602 pcie device. Some modifications to the build
system were made to enable PCIE. The test is to connect with a=20
client to the AP and run iperf (TCP). The test can run for many hours
without a problem, but sometimes fails very quickly.

The log: first the ring allocation info is printed. Starting at
16.124847, ring 2, 3 and 4 are rings used for device to host. In this
log the failure is on a read of ring 3. Ring 3 is 1024 entries of each
16 bytes. The next thing printed is the kernel page tables. Then some=20
OpenWRT info and the logging of part of the connection setup. Then at=20
1780.130752 the logging of the failure starts. The sequence number is=20
modulo 253 with ring size of 1024 matches an "old" entry (read 40,=20
expected 52). Then the different pointers are printed followed by=20
the kernel page table. The code does then a cache invalidate on the=20
dma_handle and the next read the sequence number is correct.

Regards,
Hante



